#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186092264 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186092120 :question/id #uuid "5d2c0fff-c9af-5e8a-846a-640878f08283" :question/math-academy-id "e-730" :question/problem "Find the derivative of $f(x) = \\ln(x^2-4).$" :question/worked-solution "First, we express $f(x)$ as the composite function\n\n$$\nf = \\ln u, \\qquad u = x^2-4.\n$$\n\nDifferentiating gives\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm d f}{\\textrm d u} = \\dfrac{1}{u},\\qquad \\dfrac{\\textrm{d}u}{\\textrm d x} = 2x.\n$$\n\nWe now apply the chain rule, and we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}f}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{\\text{d}f}{\\text{d}u}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{u}\\cdot 2x \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}-4}\\cdot 2x \\\\  & =\\frac{2x}{{x}^{2}-4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "b4ac7f7e-1e07-57d7-a82a-d6545588c2de" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050690 :topic/math-academy-id 1116 :topic/title "Differentiating Logarithmic Functions"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186051084 :topic/math-academy-id 1108 :topic/title "The Chain Rule for Differentiation"}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186092514 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "d52c6551-fc4a-5676-a7d0-b9c0cc8678e3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{3x^{2}}{x^{3} - 4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186089256} {:answer/id #uuid "061c6223-674b-5aec-92fb-4a26c9907ef4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{6}{x^{3} - 4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186089257} {:answer/id #uuid "1783ab77-0c34-5273-8e3b-4e40ed8e7e68" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2x\\ln (x^{3} - 4)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186089258} {:answer/id #uuid "5db05ff3-38fb-58d4-88fc-c482329f0811" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{x}{x^{3} - 4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186089259} {:answer/id #uuid "70794b27-6bac-5e9a-9c47-8ed2c45960cd" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{x^{2}}{x^{3} - 4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186089260}] :answer-field/id #uuid "3b5a8912-02dc-578f-9bd6-8084a007e4a6" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186089255}] :question/id #uuid "5e2b43c8-15e7-5f90-9659-500eb6eb563c" :question/math-academy-id "q-11934" :question/problem "Given that $f(x) = \\ln (x^{3} - 4)$, find $\\frac{df}{dx}$."} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186092677 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "8a7b4309-5fd2-5174-94f7-5dfc02eb55bc" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186090279} {:answer/id #uuid "910dd200-ce6f-5abd-8148-fc49d5edf00b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{2}{x} + 1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186090280} {:answer/id #uuid "93466ea8-4667-5103-99d9-daf38e58b163" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186090281} {:answer/id #uuid "14ee5a98-750c-5372-8840-e0127f0dc6cd" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{x} + 2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186090282} {:answer/id #uuid "498ded3b-7d79-53b4-b85c-f13be90e5ef1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{2}{x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186090283}] :answer-field/id #uuid "b9690acc-cfe2-5e00-9615-bc8aa9eb8f79" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186090278}] :question/id #uuid "4bf4a8f5-b101-5407-90c0-19deea0e2b6e" :question/math-academy-id "q-28632" :question/problem "For $g(x) = \\ln (2x)$, find $g^{′}(x)$."} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186233100 :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "e624a42e-febd-5a83-813e-03b578dd4b5f" :question/math-academy-id "q-107469" :question/problem "Find the equation of the tangent to the curve $f(x)=\\operatorname{ln}(4-2x)$ at the point $(1,\\operatorname{ln}2).$" :question/worked-solution "First, we express $f(x)$ as the composite function\n\n$$\nf=\\operatorname{ln}u,\\,u=4-2x.\n$$\n\nDifferentiating gives\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}f}{\\text{d}u}=\\frac{1}{u},\\,\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}=-2.\n$$\n\nWe now apply the chain rule, and we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}f}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{\\text{d}f}{\\text{d}u}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{u}\\cdot (-2) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4-2x}\\cdot (-2) \\\\  & =-\\frac{2}{4-2x} \\\\  & =-\\frac{\\cancel{2}}{\\cancel{2}\\cdot (2-x)} \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{2-x}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the slope $m$ of the tangent line at the point where $x=1$ is \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\frac{\\text{d}f}{\\text{d}x}|}_{x=1} & ={-\\frac{1}{2-x}|}_{x=1}=-\\frac{1}{2-1}=-1\\,.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, the slope of the tangent line at the point $(1,\\operatorname{ln}2)$ is $m=-1.$ Now, we find the equation of the tangent at that point:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y-{y}_{1} & =m(x-{x}_{1}) \\\\ y-\\operatorname{ln}2 & =-1(x-1) \\\\ y & =-x+1+\\operatorname{ln}2\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186233916 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "4001bcd4-bb92-5556-b468-857dd559531f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{30{x}^{5}}{3-5{x}^{6}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186232846}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "4001bcd4-bb92-5556-b468-857dd559531f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{30{x}^{5}}{3-5{x}^{6}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186232846} :answer-field/id #uuid "bfd8924e-61f1-5f4c-a0c0-4e1e11def7af" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186232845}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "3b0416da-07ab-52a1-b4f0-696bed0ae814" :question/math-academy-id "q-341676" :question/problem "Given that $f(x)=\\operatorname{ln}(3-5{x}^{6})$, find $\\frac{\\text{d}f}{\\text{d}x}.$\n\n$\\frac{\\text{d}f}{\\text{d}x}=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "First, we express $f(x)$ as the composite function\n\n$$\nf=\\operatorname{ln}u,\\,u=3-5{x}^{6}.\n$$\n\nDifferentiating gives\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}f}{\\text{d}u}=\\frac{1}{u},\\,\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}=-30{x}^{5}.\n$$\n\nWe now apply the chain rule, and we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}f}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{\\text{d}f}{\\text{d}u}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{u}\\cdot (-30{x}^{5}) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3-5{x}^{6}}\\cdot (-30{x}^{5}) \\\\  & =-\\frac{30{x}^{5}}{3-5{x}^{6}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186253563 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0cb5d83c-b113-5e49-8ad1-b7af79795399" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{8}{x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186250661} {:answer/id #uuid "134a924c-2d79-5a81-a5e4-b32b5f6f1568" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "8ln⁡8x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186250720} {:answer/id #uuid "6c6aaf6a-f7c4-5c7f-bd3f-349e7ca5691e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{ln⁡8x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186251444} {:answer/id #uuid "de9d5bb2-c832-5736-b2a1-be6b5d245d22" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186252513} {:answer/id #uuid "e065e44d-7317-5a6d-9dae-1e8e400b8d8d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{8x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186252529}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "de9d5bb2-c832-5736-b2a1-be6b5d245d22" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186252513} :answer-field/id #uuid "8a2d08ee-6e6a-5895-bb57-f6640a579319" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186253043}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "b9e0ca00-8b2a-5d3e-84fd-62f2c13cf2f6" :question/math-academy-id "q-11930" :question/problem "Given that $f(x)=ln⁡(8x)$, find $\\frac{\\text{d}f}{\\text{d}x}.$" :question/worked-solution "Here $f(x)=ln⁡(8x)$ is a function of a function. We use the chain rule.First, we express $g(x)$ as the composite function\n\n$$\nf=ln⁡u,\\,u=8x.\n$$\n\nDifferentiating gives\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}f}{\\text{d}u}=\\frac{1}{u},\\,\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}=8.\n$$\n\nWe now apply the chain rule, and we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}f}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{\\text{d}f}{\\text{d}u}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{u}\\cdot 8 \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{8x}\\cdot 8 \\\\  & =\\frac{8}{8x} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\cancel{8}}{\\cancel{8}x} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{x}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186253567 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2d7b3388-0623-533e-bfdf-9f2c6f502040" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2xln⁡{x}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186250941} {:answer/id #uuid "416392e1-706e-5bf2-9d81-60e36e16385e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186251095} {:answer/id #uuid "997542b7-5b4e-5730-bcd9-42ca51f3257b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{2}{{x}^{2}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186251871} {:answer/id #uuid "c6ebb09a-1202-54dc-96e3-4ba8aa79086d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{2}{x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186252309} {:answer/id #uuid "d325e97e-1656-5db8-b154-544f8ba85d31" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186252423}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "c6ebb09a-1202-54dc-96e3-4ba8aa79086d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{2}{x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186252309} :answer-field/id #uuid "49b60ab6-927a-5636-b89c-9078309c85cd" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186252934}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "ad2837f1-ee8d-5b88-b0b4-540909ba188a" :question/math-academy-id "q-11932" :question/problem "Given that $g(x)=ln⁡{x}^{2}$, find $\\frac{\\text{d}g}{\\text{d}x}.$" :question/worked-solution "First, we express $g(x)$ as the composite function\n\n$$\ng=ln⁡u,\\,u={x}^{2}.\n$$\n\nDifferentiating gives\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}g}{\\text{d}u}=\\frac{1}{u},\\,\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}=2x.\n$$\n\nWe now apply the chain rule, and we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}g}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{\\text{d}g}{\\text{d}u}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{u}\\cdot 2x \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}}\\cdot 2x \\\\  & =\\frac{2x}{{x}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{2\\cancel{x}}{x\\cdot \\cancel{x}} \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{x}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186275257 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "33827fd0-4534-55f0-97b2-d161bddcb638" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=3x+1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186272845} {:answer/id #uuid "70fa2499-450b-5a01-ac51-9c973bf1c984" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=3x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186273356} {:answer/id #uuid "9b9aa4f0-b3de-5ebd-a3bf-880c8f6adde9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186273734} {:answer/id #uuid "b22cb302-cb5a-57cd-8771-68674690aed4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=x+3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186273925} {:answer/id #uuid "b77cc92e-a825-533b-b1a3-c4323ff6da05" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=3x+\\operatorname{ln}⁡3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186273979}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "70fa2499-450b-5a01-ac51-9c973bf1c984" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=3x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186273356} :answer-field/id #uuid "95e2d1b8-e241-5d42-adc2-c7b1c34e1e9a" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186274893}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "2b8ebe67-7d90-5f2a-b18d-1cdea332312f" :question/math-academy-id "q-150014" :question/problem "Find the equation of the tangent to the curve $f(x)=\\operatorname{ln}⁡(3x+1)$ at the point $(0,0).$" :question/worked-solution "First, we express $f(x)$ as the composite function\n\n$$\nf=\\operatorname{ln}⁡u,\\,u=3x+1.\n$$\n\nDifferentiating gives\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}f}{\\text{d}u}=\\frac{1}{u},\\,\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}=3.\n$$\n\nWe now apply the chain rule, and we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}f}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{\\text{d}f}{\\text{d}u}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{u}\\cdot 3 \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3x+1}\\cdot 3 \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{3x+1}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the slope $m$ of the tangent line at the point where $x=0$ is \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\frac{\\text{d}f}{\\text{d}x}|}_{x=0} & ={\\frac{3}{3x+1}|}_{x=0} \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{3(0)+1} \\\\  & =3\\,.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, the slope of the tangent line at the point $(0,0)$ is $m=3.$ Now, we find the equation of the tangent at that point:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y-{y}_{1} & =m(x-{x}_{1}) \\\\ y-0 & =3\\cdot (x-0) \\\\ y & =3x\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186275258 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "197f7f3c-1814-5539-88cc-8d324898ee5c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=x+1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186272609} {:answer/id #uuid "2fea7db3-4e7c-5614-ae83-fa65014e2989" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=x-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186272814} {:answer/id #uuid "6bfeaa14-af54-5709-b268-7fb92c5fb931" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=-x+1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186273318} {:answer/id #uuid "a60c1e22-63e1-5366-8ce8-00afd5a3b3bf" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=-x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186273824} {:answer/id #uuid "db9385c8-9e1d-581c-b45b-9f71e6e47ebb" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=2x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186274313}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "6bfeaa14-af54-5709-b268-7fb92c5fb931" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=-x+1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186273318} :answer-field/id #uuid "a555f44f-209a-564b-9155-a69db2cccbc9" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186274923}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "329ee6a2-6455-51c9-9cfd-81f75012b6f0" :question/math-academy-id "q-150015" :question/problem "Find the equation of the tangent to the curve $f(x)=ln⁡(2-x)$ at the point $(1,0).$" :question/worked-solution "First, we express $f(x)$ as the composite function\n\n$$\nf=ln⁡u,\\,u=2-x.\n$$\n\nDifferentiating gives\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}f}{\\text{d}u}=\\frac{1}{u},\\,\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}=-1.\n$$\n\nWe now apply the chain rule, and we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}f}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{\\text{d}f}{\\text{d}u}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{u}\\cdot (-1) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2-x}\\cdot (-1) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{x-2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the slope $m$ of the tangent line at the point where $x=1$ is \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\frac{\\text{d}f}{\\text{d}x}|}_{x=1} & ={\\frac{1}{x-2}|}_{x=1} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{(1)-2} \\\\  & =-1\\,.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, the slope of the tangent line at the point $(1,0)$ is $m=-1.$ Now, we find the equation of the tangent at that point:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y-{y}_{1} & =m(x-{x}_{1}) \\\\ y-0 & =(-1)\\cdot (x-1) \\\\ y & =-x+1\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186297318 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "fd3f823c-979f-5d9f-9a75-a1d72b9ea787" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{10}{9}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186296615}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "fd3f823c-979f-5d9f-9a75-a1d72b9ea787" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{10}{9}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186296615} :answer-field/id #uuid "2796bd13-a599-55af-9fb2-de539c4e21ac" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186296708}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "f22eb9bd-4b64-597a-920f-3c1311c73e8e" :question/math-academy-id "q-310144" :question/problem "Find the slope $m$ of the tangent to the curve $f(x)=ln⁡(5{x}^{2}+4)$ at the point where $x=1.$\n\n$m=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "First, we express $f(x)$ as the composite function\n\n$$\nf=ln⁡u,\\,u=5{x}^{2}+4.\n$$\n\nDifferentiating gives\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}f}{\\text{d}u}=\\frac{1}{u},\\,\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}=10x.\n$$\n\nWe now apply the chain rule, and we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}f}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{\\text{d}f}{\\text{d}u}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{u}\\cdot 10x \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{5{x}^{2}+4}\\cdot 10x \\\\  & =\\frac{10x}{5{x}^{2}+4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the slope $m$ of the tangent line at the point where $x=1$ is \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{aligned}m=\\frac{\\text{d}f}{\\text{d}x}{|}_{x=1} & ={\\frac{10x}{5{x}^{2}+4}|}_{x=1} \\\\  & =\\frac{10(1)}{5({1}^{2})+4} \\\\  & =\\frac{10}{9}.\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186317167 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "494a94d3-24b4-54af-9812-b43bf65d1ed8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186315539}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "494a94d3-24b4-54af-9812-b43bf65d1ed8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186315539} :answer-field/id #uuid "08fcd987-267b-5991-8d3b-e7ba670ba940" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186316572}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "8869b9ad-0609-5467-9c5a-358cef5acc13" :question/math-academy-id "q-341671" :question/problem "Given that $f(x)=ln⁡(9x)$, find $\\frac{\\text{d}f}{\\text{d}x}.$\n\n$\\frac{\\text{d}f}{\\text{d}x}=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "First, we express $f(x)$ as the composite function\n\n$$\nf=ln⁡u,\\,u=9x.\n$$\n\nDifferentiating gives\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}f}{\\text{d}u}=\\frac{1}{u},\\,\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}=9.\n$$\n\nWe now apply the chain rule, and we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}f}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{\\text{d}f}{\\text{d}u}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{u}\\cdot 9 \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{9x}\\cdot 9 \\\\  & =\\frac{9}{9x} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\cancel{9}}{\\cancel{9}x} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{x}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186317168 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "41009420-72ae-56fd-aa83-eccb8bd06467" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{4}{11}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186315494}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "41009420-72ae-56fd-aa83-eccb8bd06467" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{4}{11}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186315494} :answer-field/id #uuid "72e4d41f-0ef4-5057-b7b2-fa06aa4b75c0" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186316822}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "4e7be6bd-4db9-564a-80e4-a31c43152284" :question/math-academy-id "q-341679" :question/problem "Find the slope $m$ of the tangent to the curve $f(x)=ln⁡(7-4x)$ at the point where $x=-1.$\n\n$m=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "First, we express $f(x)$ as the composite function\n\n$$\nf=ln⁡u,\\,u=7-4x.\n$$\n\nDifferentiating gives\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}f}{\\text{d}u}=\\frac{1}{u},\\,\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}=-4.\n$$\n\nWe now apply the chain rule, and we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}f}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{\\text{d}f}{\\text{d}u}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{u}\\cdot (-4) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{7-4x}\\cdot (-4) \\\\  & =-\\frac{4}{7-4x}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the slope $m$ of the tangent line at the point where $x=-1$ is \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{aligned}m=\\frac{\\text{d}f}{\\text{d}x}{|}_{x=-1} & ={-\\frac{4}{7-4x}|}_{x=-1} \\\\  & =-\\frac{4}{7-4(-1)} \\\\  & =-\\frac{4}{11}.\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186329899 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "4c9f795a-fdf7-5960-9416-86b82de61eeb" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=2x-2-ln⁡8" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186327599} {:answer/id #uuid "a92a0dde-6864-5de5-a1e0-0756332b235c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=2x-8+4ln⁡8" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186328401} {:answer/id #uuid "baa4f79f-eef4-5e8e-aac2-b20cd55f0ab6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=x-4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186328562} {:answer/id #uuid "cb545cef-1504-5ba6-adfa-85adecaf6377" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=x-4+4ln⁡8" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186328716} {:answer/id #uuid "dadc186a-82fe-5292-9424-ff20b848c7a0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=x+4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186328848}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "cb545cef-1504-5ba6-adfa-85adecaf6377" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=x-4+4ln⁡8" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186328716} :answer-field/id #uuid "61aeee27-1516-5091-a830-2d16db15c76e" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186329356}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "35dfe1cb-907d-5d3e-a83a-3ec0018cc2f7" :question/math-academy-id "q-48654" :question/problem "Find the equation of the tangent to the curve $f(x)=4ln⁡(2x)$ at the point $(4,4ln⁡8).$" :question/worked-solution "First, we express $f(x)$ as the composite function\n\n$$\nf=4ln⁡u,\\,u=2x.\n$$\n\nDifferentiating gives\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}f}{\\text{d}u}=\\frac{4}{u},\\,\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}=2.\n$$\n\nWe now apply the chain rule, and we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}f}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{\\text{d}f}{\\text{d}u}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x} \\\\  & =\\frac{4}{u}\\cdot 2 \\\\  & =\\frac{4}{2x}\\cdot 2 \\\\  & =\\frac{8}{2x} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\cancel{2}\\cdot 4}{\\cancel{2}\\cdot x} \\\\  & =\\frac{4}{x}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the slope $m$ of the tangent line at the point where $x=4$ is \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\frac{\\text{d}f}{\\text{d}x}|}_{x=4} & ={\\frac{4}{x}|}_{x=4}=\\frac{4}{4}=1\\,.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, the slope of the tangent line at the point $(4,4ln⁡8)$ is $m=1.$ Now, we find the equation of the tangent at that point:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y-{y}_{1} & =m(x-{x}_{1}) \\\\ y-4ln⁡(8) & =1(x-4) \\\\ y & =x-4+4ln⁡(8)\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186490400 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0138bb0d-e12b-5e32-a749-2cfff28eaf2a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=x - 12 + 12 \\log{\\left(24 \\right)}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186490398} {:answer/id #uuid "275a4506-33ca-5ecb-9e05-16f411b46fa5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=x + 12" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186490401} {:answer/id #uuid "3555aad0-2803-578b-8e25-d9b747f8b523" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=2 x - 6 - \\log{\\left(24 \\right)}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186490402} {:answer/id #uuid "3dd44058-54a0-5bdc-983f-368f2dce91f1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=x - 12" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186490403} {:answer/id #uuid "c5fc4c0b-607c-52ce-b3d7-ef8c8cda9424" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=2 x - 24 + 12 \\log{\\left(24 \\right)}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186490409}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "0138bb0d-e12b-5e32-a749-2cfff28eaf2a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=x - 12 + 12 \\log{\\left(24 \\right)}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186490398} :answer-field/id #uuid "e1834955-e1dd-5a91-87ec-5c78df81b919" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186490410}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "23758b8b-9bec-5ac3-b6e8-fa4ac19c5bfc" :question/problem "Find the equation of the tangent to the curve $f(x)=12\\ln(2 x)$ at the point $(12,12 \\log{\\left(24 \\right)}).$" :question/worked-solution "First, we express $f(x)$ as the composite function\n\n$$ f=12\\ln u,\\,u=2 x.$$\n\nDifferentiating gives\n\n$$\\frac{\\text{d}f}{\\text{d}u}=\\frac{12}{u},\\,\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}=2.$$\n\nWe now apply the chain rule, and we get\n\n$$\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}f}{\\text{d}x} &=\\frac{\\text{d}f}{\\text{d}u}\\cdot\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x} \\\\ &=\\frac{12}{u}\\cdot(2) \\\\ &=\\frac{12}{2 x}\\cdot(2) \\\\ &=\\frac{12}{x}.\\end{aligned}$$\n\nTherefore, the slope $m$ of the tangent line at the point where $x=12$ is\n\n$$m=\\left.(\\frac{12}{x})\\right|_{x=12}=1.$$\n\nHence, the slope of the tangent line at the point $(12,12 \\log{\\left(24 \\right)})$ is $m=1.$ Now, we find the equation of the tangent at that point:\n\n$$\\begin{aligned}y-y_1 &=m(x-x_1) \\\\ y-(12 \\log{\\left(24 \\right)}) &=(1)(x-(12)) \\\\ y &=x - 12 + 12 \\log{\\left(24 \\right)}.\\end{aligned}$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186490408 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "40aabc0b-b390-5fc8-8a17-fa253d00fd29" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=20 - x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186490404} {:answer/id #uuid "5c851fbf-5bc3-5bab-9824-5e928627f9e0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=x + 20" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186490405} {:answer/id #uuid "8d1e19b6-d29f-5051-8f57-9843249b4051" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=- x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186490406} {:answer/id #uuid "9abb9e9c-f409-58e8-935e-f307b1499f93" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=2 x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186490407} {:answer/id #uuid "e9997b57-24a6-57d1-bc84-325fbe4a7757" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=x - 20" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186490411}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "40aabc0b-b390-5fc8-8a17-fa253d00fd29" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=20 - x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186490404} :answer-field/id #uuid "1555d689-e950-59fb-a3d5-8e0733c3c608" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186490399}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "9e9f5055-f5c3-5e44-823d-b027109ff59f" :question/problem "Find the equation of the tangent to the curve $f(x)=\\ln(21 - x)$ at the point $(20,0).$" :question/worked-solution "First, we express $f(x)$ as the composite function\n\n$$ f=\\ln u,\\,u=21 - x.$$\n\nDifferentiating gives\n\n$$\\frac{\\text{d}f}{\\text{d}u}=\\frac{1}{u},\\,\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}=-1.$$\n\nWe now apply the chain rule, and we get\n\n$$\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}f}{\\text{d}x} &=\\frac{\\text{d}f}{\\text{d}u}\\cdot\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x} \\\\ &=\\frac{1}{u}\\cdot(-1) \\\\ &=\\frac{1}{21 - x}\\cdot(-1) \\\\ &=\\frac{1}{x - 21}.\\end{aligned}$$\n\nTherefore, the slope $m$ of the tangent line at the point where $x=20$ is\n\n$$m=\\left.(\\frac{1}{x - 21})\\right|_{x=20}=-1.$$\n\nHence, the slope of the tangent line at the point $(20,0)$ is $m=-1.$ Now, we find the equation of the tangent at that point:\n\n$$\\begin{aligned}y-y_1 &=m(x-x_1) \\\\ y-(0) &=(-1)(x-(20)) \\\\ y &=20 - x.\\end{aligned}$$"}] :knowledge-point/title "Differentiating a Natural Logarithm Function Using the Chain Rule"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186092265 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186092122 :question/id #uuid "7df7fed3-c172-5cc2-aca8-abd31a0902a0" :question/math-academy-id "e-731" :question/problem "If ![](images/6b/6b4554ae23fd7bb02a3dbeda7d55c342b4f30f07b57133739709048d5bfce44a.png) what is ![](images/1e/1e6f1dc22671990b694ea0386dcdcdd843ea819101a3be99a6c5378d10c5c993.png)" :question/worked-solution "First, we express ![](images/52/523ba398cc130edb20b50aa7b9a5deab0a53e4222824e3deda7f1a13ac7b0a56.png) as the composite function\n\n![](images/7d/7dc97a419c76db6b88c0862e2e54d20062ca6501657bc260eb1c478a5ad1b9eb.png)\n\nDifferentiating gives\n\n![](images/c2/c27f0454bd599e68525a774e3e0989a7ee7da4eb82701d6e918ffa334c615916.png)\n\nWe now apply the chain rule, and get\n\n![](images/70/70aaf32dd29ad000b81dcff6e4b155fe6358e923d4d1f485f71aff511233c15b.png)"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "a9102c1c-1c38-58dd-9932-b04bd24a6846" :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186092617 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "83c53678-6488-5069-a782-94aec4ff8de2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{x}\\ln 2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186089986} {:answer/id #uuid "ec75959a-0075-5d17-975e-15db4f9ae847" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2\\sqrt{x}\\ln 2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186089987} {:answer/id #uuid "21f34847-ec7a-594b-aef1-f9fd6a74fa8b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2x\\ln 2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186089988} {:answer/id #uuid "3de15677-3754-57c8-b8c3-dc66403f40f9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{x\\ln 2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186089989} {:answer/id #uuid "46df1c95-fad9-5d9f-963b-d89cb31c12eb" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186089990}] :answer-field/id #uuid "081e098c-364e-5998-afc3-0412c432aa1f" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186089985}] :question/id #uuid "1d04756c-436b-5cb9-a6bf-484f77a14eda" :question/math-academy-id "q-1948" :question/problem "Calculate $\\frac{dy}{dx}$ for $y = \\log_{2} (\\sqrt{x})$."} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186092732 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "9e532d43-a23a-5221-b598-16e29eefa128" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(5)/((2x - 5)\\ln 3)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186090593} {:answer/id #uuid "e4cc404d-6ac6-59bd-b154-17d86cfb7fe4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(1)/((5x - 2)\\ln 5)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186090594} {:answer/id #uuid "eb2d8c39-6a30-5e08-bf62-540ea07321bc" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(5)/((5x - 2)\\ln 5)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186090595} {:answer/id #uuid "f1e559ed-7a2e-5408-b30e-928c5a51cf46" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(5)/((5x - 2)\\ln 3)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186090596} {:answer/id #uuid "f2b482c8-0149-5f27-baee-354b3b6597f2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(5)/((5x - 2))" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186090597}] :answer-field/id #uuid "80eb6a81-e7b7-57ed-8210-04192f46d9f1" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186090592}] :question/id #uuid "6612d2f1-5666-54fd-9c5c-1217f3a2dfe0" :question/math-academy-id "q-48008" :question/problem "Calculate $\\frac{dy}{dx}$ for $y = \\log_{3} (5x - 2)$."} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186233614 :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "1a59875c-f4fe-5fbf-bdf0-8c6545cafda5" :question/math-academy-id "q-1949" :question/problem "Find ${y}^{'}$ for $y={log}_{5}(5x).$" :question/worked-solution "First, we express $y$ as the composite function\n\n$$\ny={log}_{5}(u),\\,u=5x.\n$$\n\nDifferentiating gives\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}u}=\\frac{1}{u\\operatorname{ln}(5)},\\,\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}=5.\n$$\n\nWe now apply the chain rule, and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}u}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{u\\operatorname{ln}(5)}\\cdot 5 \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{(5x)\\operatorname{ln}(5)}\\cdot 5 \\\\  & =\\frac{\\cancel{5}}{\\cancel{5}x\\operatorname{ln}(5)} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{x\\operatorname{ln}(5)}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186233771 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "5934d9e7-e0c8-5458-86ce-4c4763e0c2f2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{6}{(6x-7)\\operatorname{ln}(5)}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186232534}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "5934d9e7-e0c8-5458-86ce-4c4763e0c2f2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{6}{(6x-7)\\operatorname{ln}(5)}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186232534} :answer-field/id #uuid "5ac5ce9e-8f0b-54f1-8df9-3cdb343bd31a" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186232533}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "e3069ad7-4f7f-5df7-a773-d5a4a2ddea34" :question/math-academy-id "q-310148" :question/problem "Given that $f(x)={log}_{5}(7-6x),$ find ${f}^{'}(x).$\n\n${f}^{'}(x)=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "First, we express $f(x)$ as the composite function\n\n$$\n{f}^{'}(x)={log}_{5}(u),\\,u=7-6x.\n$$\n\nDifferentiating gives \n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}f}{\\text{d}u}=\\frac{1}{u\\operatorname{ln}(5)},\\,\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}=-6.\n$$\n\nWe now apply the chain rule, and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}f}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{\\text{d}f}{\\text{d}u}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{u\\operatorname{ln}(5)}\\cdot (-6) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{(7-6x)\\operatorname{ln}(5)}\\cdot (-6) \\\\  & =\\frac{6}{(6x-7)\\operatorname{ln}(5)}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186233917 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/id #uuid "c9eaeba2-e2db-5a16-9e21-e70aff99fec1" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186232847}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "59b04652-c0de-5556-8dc3-5d8cbfbc7a11" :question/math-academy-id "q-341686" :question/problem "Given that $f(x)={log}_{7}(4x-9),$ find ${f}^{'}(x).$\n\n${f}^{'}(x)=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "First, we express $f(x)$ as the composite function\n\n$$\n{f}^{'}(x)={log}_{7}(u),\\,u=4x-9.\n$$\n\nDifferentiating gives \n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}f}{\\text{d}u}=\\frac{1}{u\\operatorname{ln}(7)},\\,\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}=4.\n$$\n\nWe now apply the chain rule, and get \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}f}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{\\text{d}f}{\\text{d}u}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{u\\operatorname{ln}(7)}\\cdot 4 \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{(4x-9)\\operatorname{ln}(7)}\\cdot 4 \\\\  & =\\frac{4}{(4x-9)\\operatorname{ln}(7)}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186233918 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "ca7c933c-200f-5c04-9521-252614047222" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{x\\operatorname{ln}(10)}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186232849}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "ca7c933c-200f-5c04-9521-252614047222" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{x\\operatorname{ln}(10)}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186232849} :answer-field/id #uuid "42bf3c39-5bed-5d63-8c10-8ba4f9ce3eb4" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186232848}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "735a6508-93d8-5546-a8c1-3d1b658908d1" :question/math-academy-id "q-341688" :question/problem "Given that $f(x)=\\operatorname{log}(8x),$ find ${f}^{'}(x).$\n\n${f}^{'}(x)=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "First, we express $f(x)$ as the composite function\n\n$$\n{f}^{'}(x)=\\operatorname{log}(u),\\,u=8x.\n$$\n\nDifferentiating gives \n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}f}{\\text{d}u}=\\frac{1}{u\\operatorname{ln}(10)},\\,\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}=8.\n$$\n\nWe now apply the chain rule, and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}f}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{\\text{d}f}{\\text{d}u}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{u\\operatorname{ln}(10)}\\cdot 8 \\\\  & =\\frac{8}{8x\\operatorname{ln}(10)} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\cancel{8}}{\\cancel{8}x\\operatorname{ln}(10)} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{x\\operatorname{ln}(10)}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186234085 :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "4535d094-b93c-5a3e-b232-f228d01581bb" :question/math-academy-id "q-48001" :question/problem "Find ${f}^{'}(x)$ for $f(x)={log}_{2}(3-2x).$" :question/worked-solution "First, we express $f(x)$ as the composite function\n\n$$\n{f}^{'}(x)={log}_{2}(u),\\,u=3-2x.\n$$\n\nDifferentiating gives\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}f}{\\text{d}u}=\\frac{1}{u\\operatorname{ln}(2)},\\,\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}=-2.\n$$\n\nWe now apply the chain rule, and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}f}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{\\text{d}f}{\\text{d}u}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{u\\operatorname{ln}(2)}\\cdot (-2) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{(3-2x)\\operatorname{ln}(2)}\\cdot (-2) \\\\  & =-\\frac{2}{(3-2x)\\operatorname{ln}(2)}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186234306 :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "b9232cab-7b8f-54c3-8a86-8363101afe46" :question/math-academy-id "q-71479" :question/problem "Find ${y}^{'}$ for $y={log}_{4}(2-5x).$" :question/worked-solution "First, we express $y$ as the composite function\n\n$$\ny={log}_{4}(u),\\,u=2-5x.\n$$\n\nDifferentiating gives\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}u}=\\frac{1}{u\\operatorname{ln}(4)},\\,\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}=-5.\n$$\n\nWe now apply the chain rule, and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}u}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{u\\operatorname{ln}(4)}\\cdot (-5) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{(2-5x)\\operatorname{ln}(4)}\\cdot (-5) \\\\  & =-\\frac{5}{(2-5x)\\operatorname{ln}(4)}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186297319 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "35e1e123-6d5e-55d1-b3f7-259ea04d1490" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{3}{2\\operatorname{ln}(4)}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186295570}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "35e1e123-6d5e-55d1-b3f7-259ea04d1490" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{3}{2\\operatorname{ln}(4)}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186295570} :answer-field/id #uuid "5daac6b9-af4b-5226-9dc7-fcd1d59fa479" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186296825}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "d9148bcf-019e-5ab0-a8b9-eb415bd0a6d1" :question/math-academy-id "q-310145" :question/problem "Find the slope $m$ of the tangent to the curve $y={log}_{4}⁡(6x-2)+7$ at the point where $x=1.$\n\n$m=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "First, we express $y$ as the composite function\n\n$$\ny={log}_{4}⁡(u)+7,\\,u=6x-2.\n$$\n\nDifferentiating gives\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}u}=\\frac{1}{uln⁡(4)},\\,\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}=6.\n$$\n\nWe now apply the chain rule, and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}u}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{uln⁡(4)}\\cdot 6 \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{(6x-2)ln⁡(4)}\\cdot 6 \\\\  & =\\frac{6}{(6x-2)ln⁡(4)} \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{(3x-1)ln⁡(4)}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the slope $m$ of the tangent line at the point where $x=1$ is \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{aligned}m=\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}{|}_{x=1} & =\\frac{3}{(3x-1)ln⁡(4)}{|}_{x=1} \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{(3(1)-1)ln⁡(4)} \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{2ln⁡(4)}.\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186297320 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "ab4c8814-91db-509e-be0f-19786b9a5b04" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{5{x}^{4}}{({x}^{5}-2)\\operatorname{ln}(10)}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186296165}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "ab4c8814-91db-509e-be0f-19786b9a5b04" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{5{x}^{4}}{({x}^{5}-2)\\operatorname{ln}(10)}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186296165} :answer-field/id #uuid "f7a3e276-476f-5c15-b0cf-fc02a349ee68" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186297142}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "c7331908-3e29-5baf-bb7e-ba78549a5c63" :question/math-academy-id "q-310149" :question/problem "Given that $y=log⁡({x}^{5}-2),$ find $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}$ in terms of $x.$\n\n$\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "First, we express $y$ as the composite function\n\n$$\ny=log⁡(u),\\,u={x}^{5}-2.\n$$\n\nDifferentiating gives\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}u}=\\frac{1}{uln⁡(10)},\\,\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}=5{x}^{4}.\n$$\n\nWe now apply the chain rule, and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}u}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{uln⁡(10)}\\cdot 5{x}^{4} \\\\  & =\\frac{5{x}^{4}}{({x}^{5}-2)ln⁡(10)}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186317170 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "c30e987d-300b-5bb7-8870-858f7f0c30f0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{3}{x\\operatorname{ln}(6)}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186316214}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "c30e987d-300b-5bb7-8870-858f7f0c30f0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{3}{x\\operatorname{ln}(6)}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186316214} :answer-field/id #uuid "2b627fb6-d94c-5d7d-94bd-7c876b01da4b" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186316646}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "168ec5f7-4b56-507b-951f-2b0c361acc27" :question/math-academy-id "q-341691" :question/problem "Given that $y={log}_{6}⁡(-3{x}^{3}),$ find $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}$ in terms of $x.$\n\n$\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "First, we express $y$ as the composite function\n\n$$\ny={log}_{6}⁡(u),\\,u=-3{x}^{3}.\n$$\n\nDifferentiating gives\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}u}=\\frac{1}{uln⁡(6)},\\,\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}=-9{x}^{2}.\n$$\n\nWe now apply the chain rule, and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}u}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{uln⁡(6)}\\cdot (-9{x}^{2}) \\\\  & =\\frac{-9{x}^{2}}{-3{x}^{3}ln⁡(6)} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\cancel{-3{x}^{2}}\\cdot 3}{\\cancel{-3{x}^{2}}\\cdot xln⁡(6)} \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{xln⁡(6)}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186317171 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2ad63f63-132c-5fda-bb04-ef5f4af5d49e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2x\\operatorname{ln}(4)}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186315367}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "2ad63f63-132c-5fda-bb04-ef5f4af5d49e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2x\\operatorname{ln}(4)}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186315367} :answer-field/id #uuid "dd0a85d2-2577-5653-a179-cca94c0325c1" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186317074}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "d47d4d4a-5112-5202-876b-f3e480a31b61" :question/math-academy-id "q-341694" :question/problem "Given that $y={log}_{4}⁡(\\sqrt{7x}),$ find $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}$  in terms of $x.$\n\n$\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "First, we express $y$ as the composite function\n\n$$\ny={log}_{4}⁡(u),\\,u=\\sqrt{7x}.\n$$\n\nDifferentiating gives\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}u}=\\frac{1}{uln⁡(4)},\\,\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{7}{2\\sqrt{7x}}.\n$$\n\nWe now apply the chain rule, and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}u}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{uln⁡(4)}\\cdot \\frac{7}{2\\sqrt{7x}} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{7x}ln⁡(4)}\\cdot \\frac{7}{2\\sqrt{7x}} \\\\  & =\\frac{7}{2\\cdot 7xln⁡(4)} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\cancel{7}}{2\\cdot \\cancel{7}xln⁡(4)} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2xln⁡(4)}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186329895 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "79f13dd8-26c9-5adf-8bdd-6c74fc06256d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{{x}^{3}ln⁡(2)}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186327980} {:answer/id #uuid "8b93ae68-75e8-5e22-9480-dd13fcb145cf" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{3}{x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186328133} {:answer/id #uuid "9f823260-520c-55a0-b92f-e5fd464f4ac6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{3}{xln⁡(2)}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186328312} {:answer/id #uuid "a3e3c6b3-2aba-508b-9ed5-660969c16420" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{3}{{x}^{2}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186328353} {:answer/id #uuid "ef8c97b0-8b83-5367-985e-2d436e277e28" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{3xln⁡(2)}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186329026}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "9f823260-520c-55a0-b92f-e5fd464f4ac6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{3}{xln⁡(2)}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186328312} :answer-field/id #uuid "9056d537-4120-54a4-91a1-0704cebf5089" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186329423}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "288c307e-b8cb-5e83-b4ba-4f7abf6cf59d" :question/math-academy-id "q-48559" :question/problem "Given that $y={log}_{2}⁡({x}^{3}),$ find $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}.$" :question/worked-solution "First, we express $y$ as the composite function\n\n$$\ny={log}_{2}⁡(u),\\,u={x}^{3}.\n$$\n\nDifferentiating gives\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}u}=\\frac{1}{uln⁡(2)},\\,\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}=3{x}^{2}.\n$$\n\nWe now apply the chain rule, and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}u}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{uln⁡(2)}\\cdot 3{x}^{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{3{x}^{2}}{{x}^{3}ln⁡(2)} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\cancel{{x}^{2}}\\cdot 3}{\\cancel{{x}^{2}}\\cdot xln⁡(2)} \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{xln⁡(2)}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186329900 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "5cd88001-594a-5d1f-b80b-a2b258386cbc" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{5}{ln⁡(3)}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186327751} {:answer/id #uuid "9a80f11d-085c-5ea7-ab96-6ce21f491dc7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{5}{3ln⁡(3)}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186328270} {:answer/id #uuid "9ee8c8de-94f2-593c-9518-5dde2376aefa" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{3}{5ln⁡(3)}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186328306} {:answer/id #uuid "ccd2202c-ab6e-5f06-8a86-6652d3d0e787" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{15}{ln⁡(3)}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186328731} {:answer/id #uuid "db9b7a29-e7bc-51c7-a0e1-df492323b2af" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{3}{ln⁡(3)}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186328855}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "ccd2202c-ab6e-5f06-8a86-6652d3d0e787" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{15}{ln⁡(3)}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186328731} :answer-field/id #uuid "5a14bf32-63c1-5362-83c1-dfaf55039036" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186329346}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "e13df585-bbb8-5747-9099-5dc67c6a041d" :question/math-academy-id "q-48656" :question/problem "Find the slope of the tangent to the curve $y={log}_{3}⁡(5x-2)+5$ at the point where $x=\\frac{7}{15}.$" :question/worked-solution "First, we express $y$ as the composite function\n\n$$\ny={log}_{3}⁡(u)+5,\\,u=5x-2.\n$$\n\nDifferentiating gives\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}u}=\\frac{1}{uln⁡(3)},\\,\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}=5.\n$$\n\nWe now apply the chain rule, and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}u}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{uln⁡(3)}\\cdot 5 \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{(5x-2)ln⁡(3)}\\cdot 5 \\\\  & =\\frac{5}{(5x-2)ln⁡(3)}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the slope $m$ of the tangent line at the point where $x=\\frac{7}{15}$ is \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{aligned}m=\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}{|}_{x=7/15} & =\\frac{5}{(5x-2)ln⁡(3)}{|}_{x=7/15} \\\\  & =\\frac{5}{(5(\\frac{7}{15})-2)ln⁡(3)} \\\\  & =\\frac{5}{\\frac{1}{3}ln⁡(3)} \\\\  & =\\frac{15}{ln⁡(3)}.\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186332869 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1040edda-0303-5346-ac49-2025a711486e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{1}{(1-x)ln⁡(10)}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186330092} {:answer/id #uuid "1b3385ec-93b4-59c7-a196-58be5d17d757" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{10}{(1-x)}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186330189} {:answer/id #uuid "430a86b3-46fa-5177-b8b1-9abf9afaa40a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{1}{(1-x)}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186330550} {:answer/id #uuid "6e2c3511-369b-5b02-8467-198f1bcf8279" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(1-x)ln⁡(10)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186330941} {:answer/id #uuid "afdc0472-7689-57f5-b5c2-148cb66217a8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{ln⁡(10)}{(1-x)}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186331507}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "1040edda-0303-5346-ac49-2025a711486e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{1}{(1-x)ln⁡(10)}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186330092} :answer-field/id #uuid "85a34950-0b5d-5d67-b7f8-2bcede974234" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186332436}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "0c643137-d804-5b6f-93e8-568de57f1d93" :question/math-academy-id "q-51317" :question/problem "Find ${y}^{′}$ for $y=log⁡(1-x).$" :question/worked-solution "First, we express $y$ as the composite function\n\n$$\ny=log⁡u={log}_{10}⁡(u),\\,u=1-x.\n$$\n\nDifferentiating gives\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}u}=\\frac{1}{uln⁡(10)},\\,\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}=-1.\n$$\n\nWe now apply the chain rule, and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}u}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{uln⁡(10)}\\cdot (-1) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{(1-x)ln⁡(10)}\\cdot (-1) \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{(1-x)ln⁡(10)}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186338953 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "01b8e9c7-5d13-5a8b-b24a-bc0f66b920f6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{xln⁡(3)}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186336047} {:answer/id #uuid "9952e4d6-b2f6-5350-968d-96c8aba103d2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186337427} {:answer/id #uuid "b68141cd-2dc9-5161-b824-9e26089c595b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{2}{x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186337653} {:answer/id #uuid "e8e79c63-e3c3-5370-90dc-730b8ce5ad45" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{2}{xln⁡(3)}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186338063} {:answer/id #uuid "f4d6f5a4-026a-5249-9836-8e276315d0e1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2xln⁡(3)}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186338172}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "f4d6f5a4-026a-5249-9836-8e276315d0e1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2xln⁡(3)}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186338172} :answer-field/id #uuid "8dd6219f-fa77-5c82-9c67-5c75b2b8f9e9" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186338539}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "aa9dfcc3-c12e-5f8e-a4b8-990237278f4d" :question/math-academy-id "q-71478" :question/problem "Calculate $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}$ for $y={log}_{3}⁡(\\sqrt{2x}).$" :question/worked-solution "First, we express $y$ as the composite function\n\n$$\ny={log}_{3}⁡(u),\\,u=\\sqrt{2x}.\n$$\n\nDifferentiating gives\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}u}=\\frac{1}{uln⁡(3)},\\,\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2\\sqrt{x}}=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2x}}.\n$$\n\nWe now apply the chain rule, and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}u}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{uln⁡(3)}\\cdot \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2x}} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2x}ln⁡(3)}\\cdot \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2x}} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2xln⁡(3)}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] :knowledge-point/title "Differentiating a General Logarithm Function Using the Chain Rule"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186175923 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186175737 :question/id #uuid "ca9ec8da-5f74-53dc-afa7-e6cc965fcc95" :question/math-academy-id "e-14363" :question/problem "Find the equation of the line that passes through the point $(3,4)$ and is perpendicular to the line passing through the points $(0,1)$ and $(-2,4).$" :question/worked-solution "Perpendicular lines have slopes which are negative reciprocals of each other.\n\nFirst, let's find the slope $m_1$ of the line passing through the points $(0,1)$ and $(-2,4)\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{m}_{1} & =\\frac{{y}_{2}-{y}_{1}}{{x}_{2}-{x}_{1}} \\\\  & =\\frac{4-1}{-2-0} \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{-2} \\\\  & =-\\frac{3}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince we want a perpendicular line, the slope $m$ of the line we're trying to find must satisfy\n\n$$\nm \\cdot \\left(-\\dfrac{3}{2}\\right) = -1 \\quad \\Rightarrow \\quad m = \\dfrac{2}{3}.\n$$\n\nWe know that the line passes through the point $(3,4).$ Using point-slope form and simplifying, we obtain the equation of the line:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y-{y}_{1} & =m(x-{x}_{1}) \\\\ y-4 & =\\frac{2}{3}(x-3) \\\\ y-4 & =\\frac{2}{3}x-2 \\\\ y & =\\frac{2}{3}x+2\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "44b9b212-27c4-5d6d-a221-cd85c25fc956" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049061 :topic/math-academy-id 396 :topic/title "Calculating Slopes of Straight Lines"}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186176418 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/id #uuid "4b68033b-a4e7-5f65-b638-4450f1d5a103" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186174741}] :question/id #uuid "d3eb7ad1-efad-5cfc-9def-a4730d00ac8c" :question/math-academy-id "q-214038" :question/problem "What is the equation of the line that passes through the point $(6,-5)$ and is perpendicular to the line passing through the points $(2, 5)$ and $(7, 3)$?$y =$\n{{field-1}}"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186176429 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "8c6f703b-eaf6-555f-839c-9af87138f4f4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y =-\\frac{1}{5}x + 5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186174755} {:answer/id #uuid "aff9fd7a-670e-5495-8397-7ce141521dec" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y = \\frac{6}{5}x + 5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186174756} {:answer/id #uuid "3af724d4-8486-55c0-80a0-48cfab90e646" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y =-\\frac{6}{5}x + 5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186174757} {:answer/id #uuid "5014213f-3b1a-5ad8-8f00-3c24163d61cb" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y = \\frac{5}{6}x + 5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186174758} {:answer/id #uuid "647e66bd-ab6f-558e-92de-5849d1064776" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y =-\\frac{5}{6}x + 5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186174759}] :answer-field/id #uuid "af060219-3ae1-5711-9156-cac1c55d3e91" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186174754}] :question/id #uuid "42d56d4c-2843-5563-82a0-0618b673d148" :question/math-academy-id "q-22642" :question/problem "Find the equation of the line that passes through the point $(0, 5)$ and is perpendicular to the line passing through the points $(-2,-4)$ and $(3, 2)$."} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186235495 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "c3c9ed58-392f-5223-bc99-c5d9072bf047" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{3}x-6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186234851}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "c3c9ed58-392f-5223-bc99-c5d9072bf047" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{3}x-6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186234851} :answer-field/id #uuid "b7c93770-8bbc-5afb-aed5-b4b4b6387392" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186235197}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "f2753316-4b68-5a78-b169-53e3c0eb33f7" :question/math-academy-id "q-167797" :question/problem "Write the equation of the line that passes through the point $(3,-5)$ and is perpendicular to $\\overset{↔}{AB}.$\n\n                            \n                                    \n                                        \n                                            \n\n                                                \n\n                                        \n                                        \n                                        \n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                                        \n                                        The slope-intercept form of the equation is $y\\,=\\,$\n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                            \n                                        {{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "Perpendicular lines have slopes that are negative reciprocals of each other. Therefore, since $\\overset{↔}{AB}$ has the slope $m=-3,$ our perpendicular line must have a slope of \n\n$$\n{m}_{1}=-\\frac{1}{m}=-\\frac{1}{(-3)}=\\frac{1}{3}.\n$$\n\nSo, our perpendicular line has the slope ${m}_{1}=\\frac{1}{3}$ and passes through the point $({x}_{1},{y}_{1})=(3,-5).$ \n\nFinally, using point-slope form and simplifying, we obtain the following equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y-{y}_{1} & ={m}_{1}(x-{x}_{1}) \\\\ y-(-5) & =\\frac{1}{3}(x-3) \\\\ y+5 & =\\frac{1}{3}x-1 \\\\ y & =\\frac{1}{3}x-6\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186238007 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "35533677-024f-5015-b5fc-c9c35bcd97a4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=-\\frac{2}{5}x+\\frac{19}{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186236155} {:answer/id #uuid "461eb43d-23b5-5be8-b5eb-d1535f26ab59" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=\\frac{5}{2}x-2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186236269} {:answer/id #uuid "8c418cf2-df35-513d-86a0-953d17273ccb" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=-\\frac{5}{2}x+8" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186236703} {:answer/id #uuid "c25d730c-740a-5089-a348-d56552dfbfad" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=\\frac{1}{2}x+2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186237047} {:answer/id #uuid "f31cf18d-2ca6-52a8-ae8f-44129d24abf1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=\\frac{2}{5}x+\\frac{11}{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186237350}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "461eb43d-23b5-5be8-b5eb-d1535f26ab59" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=\\frac{5}{2}x-2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186236269} :answer-field/id #uuid "ae135409-b9c1-54f7-b3eb-0d0be03cf77d" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186237662}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "758e4600-77be-53f9-a21f-0c9a0a5da17f" :question/math-academy-id "q-103440" :question/problem "Find the equation of the line that passes through the point $(2,3)$ and is perpendicular to the line passing through the points $(1,4)$ and $(-4,6).$" :question/worked-solution "Perpendicular lines have slopes that are negative reciprocals of each other.\n\nFirst, let's find the slope ${m}_{1}$ of the line passing through the points $(1,4)$ and $(-4,6):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{m}_{1} & =\\frac{{y}_{2}-{y}_{1}}{{x}_{2}-{x}_{1}} \\\\  & =\\frac{6-4}{-4-1} \\\\  & =-\\frac{2}{5}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince we want a perpendicular line, the slope $m$ of the line we're trying to find must satisfy\n\n$$\nm\\cdot (-\\frac{2}{5})=-1\\,⇒\\,m=\\frac{5}{2}.\n$$\n\nWe know that the line passes through the point $(2,3).$ Using point-slope form and simplifying, we obtain the equation of the line:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y-{y}_{1} & =m(x-{x}_{1}) \\\\ y-3 & =\\frac{5}{2}(x-2) \\\\ y-3 & =\\frac{5}{2}x-5 \\\\ y & =\\frac{5}{2}x-2\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186238010 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1f907072-63f6-5f5f-8f48-b581ee8d9964" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=-\\frac{4}{5}x+9" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186236017} {:answer/id #uuid "679b6c28-4249-5434-8796-946eedec1017" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=\\frac{5}{4}x-11" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186236471} {:answer/id #uuid "9fbc543c-735d-56bd-91ca-9de8c441f245" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=-\\frac{5}{2}x+19" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186236814} {:answer/id #uuid "a80fa7f7-5d9d-58db-96e1-7ccc0ab7f96d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=\\frac{4}{5}x-9" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186236869} {:answer/id #uuid "b6f63932-6678-5b50-ad61-c13da4cdb606" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=-\\frac{5}{4}x+9" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186236977}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "679b6c28-4249-5434-8796-946eedec1017" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=\\frac{5}{4}x-11" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186236471} :answer-field/id #uuid "c87be670-f763-5d7b-8ef2-17d4e48d4561" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186237697}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "c4caa1a7-6407-5b10-be10-2977d4647c3d" :question/math-academy-id "q-103455" :question/problem "Find the equation of the line that passes through the point $(8,-1)$ and is perpendicular to the line passing through the points $(-4,1)$ and $(1,-3).$" :question/worked-solution "Perpendicular lines have slopes that are negative reciprocals of each other.\n\nFirst, let's find the slope ${m}_{1}$ of the line passing through the points $(-4,1)$ and $(1,-3):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{m}_{1} & =\\frac{{y}_{2}-{y}_{1}}{{x}_{2}-{x}_{1}} \\\\  & =\\frac{-3-1}{1-(-4)} \\\\  & =\\frac{-3-1}{1+4} \\\\  & =-\\frac{4}{5}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince we want a perpendicular line, the slope $m$ of the line we're trying to find must satisfy\n\n$$\nm\\cdot (-\\frac{4}{5})=-1\\,⇒\\,m=\\frac{5}{4}.\n$$\n\nWe know that the line passes through the point $(8,-1).$ Using point-slope form and simplifying, we obtain the equation of the line:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y-{y}_{1} & =m(x-{x}_{1}) \\\\ y-(-1) & =\\frac{5}{4}(x-8) \\\\ y+1 & =\\frac{5}{4}x-10 \\\\ y & =\\frac{5}{4}x-11\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186290316 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "57533672-f7c9-578c-bd03-fcbcba3dcbbc" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x+4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186288638}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "57533672-f7c9-578c-bd03-fcbcba3dcbbc" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x+4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186288638} :answer-field/id #uuid "1a84cf4d-a7d6-504e-983d-f05875b4e775" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186289794}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "55729d30-da9a-5863-9116-cd05f93a8d4a" :question/math-academy-id "q-215768" :question/problem "What is the equation of the line that passes through the point $(0,4)$ and is perpendicular to the line passing through the points $(-5,1)$ and $(-6,2)?$\n\n$y=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "Perpendicular lines have slopes that are negative reciprocals of each other.\n\nFirst, let's find the slope ${m}_{1}$ of the line passing through the points $(-5,1)$ and $(-6,2):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{m}_{1} & =\\frac{{y}_{2}-{y}_{1}}{{x}_{2}-{x}_{1}} \\\\  & =\\frac{2-1}{-6-(-5)} \\\\  & =\\frac{2-1}{-6+5} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{-1} \\\\  & =-1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince we want a perpendicular line, the slope $m$ of the line we're trying to find must satisfy\n\n$$\nm\\cdot (-1)=-1\\,⇒\\,m=1.\n$$\n\nWe know that the line passes through the point $(0,4).$ Using point-slope form and simplifying, we obtain the equation of the line in slope-intercept form:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y-{y}_{1} & =m(x-{x}_{1}) \\\\ y-4 & =1(x-0) \\\\ y-4 & =x \\\\ y & =x+4\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186290460 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1024e4c0-bbcd-5e61-84f8-3dff5e50f5d2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=\\frac{7}{3}x-3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186288154} {:answer/id #uuid "297e02ff-5dc0-5fd3-8271-aee2c38fccd7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=-\\frac{3}{4}x-3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186288308} {:answer/id #uuid "6a24547b-b78e-5d0e-90b1-33cec5f86ea3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=5x-3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186288781} {:answer/id #uuid "c3fb0467-1a97-512a-aa0f-150efb576a10" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=-2x-3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186289326} {:answer/id #uuid "c4988bec-b2bd-5011-9ca7-75d333c159db" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=\\frac{4}{3}x-3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186289332}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "297e02ff-5dc0-5fd3-8271-aee2c38fccd7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=-\\frac{3}{4}x-3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186288308} :answer-field/id #uuid "c865f6fa-cf35-513a-a3d6-3e960c11b6b1" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186290166}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "e6cef704-dc0c-5ab7-9bc9-e3a20f725642" :question/math-academy-id "q-23453" :question/problem "Find the equation of the line that passes through the point $(0,-3)$ and is perpendicular to the line passing through the points $(1,7)$ and $(-5,-1).$" :question/worked-solution "Perpendicular lines have slopes which are negative reciprocals of each other.\n\nFirst, let's find the slope ${m}_{1}$ of the line passing through the points $(1,7)$ and $(-5,-1):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{m}_{1} & =\\frac{{y}_{2}-{y}_{1}}{{x}_{2}-{x}_{1}} \\\\  & =\\frac{-1-7}{-5-1} \\\\  & =\\frac{-8}{-6} \\\\  & =\\frac{4}{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince we want a perpendicular line, the slope $m$ of the line we're trying to find must satisfy\n\n$$\nm\\cdot (\\frac{4}{3})=-1\\,⇒\\,m=-\\frac{3}{4}.\n$$\n\nWe know that the line passes through the point $(0,-3).$ Using point-slope form and simplifying, we obtain the equation of the line:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y-{y}_{1} & =m(x-{x}_{1}) \\\\ y-(-3) & =-\\frac{3}{4}(x-0) \\\\ y+3 & =-\\frac{3}{4}x \\\\ y & =-\\frac{3}{4}x-3\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186290495 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "e701b8f4-7d6b-5c45-b082-b976895be196" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186289564}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "e701b8f4-7d6b-5c45-b082-b976895be196" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186289564} :answer-field/id #uuid "de6596ac-82a2-591c-bfcf-0cf47b94c5ae" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186290207}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "8c855e16-f691-5fbd-bcf1-319b489f96c6" :question/math-academy-id "q-237036" :question/problem "Find the equation of the line that passes through the point $(-5,5)$ and is perpendicular to the line passing through the points $(-4,-3)$ and $(2,3).$\n\n$y=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "Perpendicular lines have slopes that are negative reciprocals of each other.\n\nFirst, let's find the slope ${m}_{1}$ of the line passing through the points $(-4,-3)$ and $(2,3):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{m}_{1} & =\\frac{{y}_{2}-{y}_{1}}{{x}_{2}-{x}_{1}} \\\\  & =\\frac{3-(-3)}{2-(-4)} \\\\  & =\\frac{6}{6} \\\\  & =1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince we want a perpendicular line, the slope $m$ of the line we're trying to find must satisfy\n\n$$\nm\\cdot (1)=-1\\,⇒\\,m=-1.\n$$\n\nWe know that the line passes through the point $(-5,5).$ Using point-slope form and simplifying, we obtain the equation of the line in slope-intercept form:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y-{y}_{1} & =m(x-{x}_{1}) \\\\ y-5 & =-1(x-(-5)) \\\\ y-5 & =-1(x+5) \\\\ y-5 & =-x-5 \\\\ y & =-x\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186290498 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "701eb222-0215-5e1e-aeed-f86ef2b1e8b6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{6}{5}x-2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186288811}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "701eb222-0215-5e1e-aeed-f86ef2b1e8b6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{6}{5}x-2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186288811} :answer-field/id #uuid "57e36efb-7123-5c18-8518-75c3551a7b86" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186289915}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "7172ccc4-2a2a-5ae6-a89d-55424e3bf138" :question/math-academy-id "q-237224" :question/problem "What is the equation of the line that passes through the point $(0,-2)$ and is perpendicular to the line passing through the points $(-2,4)$ and $(4,-1)?$\n\n$y=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "Perpendicular lines have slopes that are negative reciprocals of each other.\n\nFirst, let's find the slope ${m}_{1}$ of the line passing through the points $(-2,4)$ and $(4,-1):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{m}_{1} & =\\frac{{y}_{2}-{y}_{1}}{{x}_{2}-{x}_{1}} \\\\  & =\\frac{-1-4}{4-(-2)} \\\\  & =\\frac{-1-4}{4+2} \\\\  & =-\\frac{5}{6}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince we want a perpendicular line, the slope $m$ of the line we're trying to find must satisfy\n\n$$\nm\\cdot (-\\frac{5}{6})=-1\\,⇒\\,m=\\frac{6}{5}.\n$$\n\nWe know that the line passes through the point $(0,-2).$ Using point-slope form and simplifying, we obtain the equation of the line in slope-intercept form:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y-{y}_{1} & =m(x-{x}_{1}) \\\\ y-(-2) & =\\frac{6}{5}(x-0) \\\\ y+2 & =\\frac{6}{5}x \\\\ y & =\\frac{6}{5}x-2\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186290499 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2deda78d-e190-5ac6-8f09-dfdafe1448fe" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{2}{7}x-5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186288341}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "2deda78d-e190-5ac6-8f09-dfdafe1448fe" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{2}{7}x-5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186288341} :answer-field/id #uuid "5fb2bc39-aed8-54cc-8f5c-a5e009a5e97a" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186289937}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "01d34255-de74-5cbb-862e-52884bec6cfd" :question/math-academy-id "q-237225" :question/problem "What is the equation of the line that passes through the point $(0,-5)$ and is perpendicular to the line passing through the points $(1,-3)$ and $(3,4)?$\n\n$y=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "Perpendicular lines have slopes that are negative reciprocals of each other.\n\nFirst, let's find the slope ${m}_{1}$ of the line passing through the points $(1,-3)$ and $(3,4):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{m}_{1} & =\\frac{{y}_{2}-{y}_{1}}{{x}_{2}-{x}_{1}} \\\\  & =\\frac{4-(-3)}{3-1} \\\\  & =\\frac{4+3}{3-1} \\\\  & =\\frac{7}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince we want a perpendicular line, the slope $m$ of the line we're trying to find must satisfy\n\n$$\nm\\cdot (\\frac{7}{2})=-1\\,⇒\\,m=-\\frac{2}{7}.\n$$\n\nWe know that the line passes through the point $(0,-5).$ Using point-slope form and simplifying, we obtain the equation of the line in slope-intercept form:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y-{y}_{1} & =m(x-{x}_{1}) \\\\ y-(-5) & =-\\frac{2}{7}(x-0) \\\\ y+5 & =-\\frac{2}{7}x \\\\ y & =-\\frac{2}{7}x-5\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186339134 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0a66ab68-36c3-5c49-97a3-eba1003dd886" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=-3x-13" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186336128} {:answer/id #uuid "32cee572-2261-55af-9018-9c7a204018f4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=3x+6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186336514} {:answer/id #uuid "59ad4d32-5750-5654-afec-1553cc4b52bd" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=\\frac{1}{6}x+6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186336851} {:answer/id #uuid "972da17b-34f0-585d-920b-18729c1e5078" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=-\\frac{1}{6}x+4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186337408} {:answer/id #uuid "dec392c4-3ead-52cd-a574-74279c757e6a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=\\frac{1}{6}x-3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186337974}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "59ad4d32-5750-5654-afec-1553cc4b52bd" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=\\frac{1}{6}x+6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186336851} :answer-field/id #uuid "8e485dbb-42bf-52c7-84d1-8c3ba7c006ad" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186338541}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "91f3a022-c4bb-5e42-8f8c-ad041616b931" :question/math-academy-id "q-72567" :question/problem "Find the equation of the line that passes through the point $(-6,5)$ and is perpendicular to the line passing through the points $(-2,6)$ and $(0,-6).$" :question/worked-solution "Perpendicular lines have slopes which are negative reciprocals of each other.\n\nFirst, let's find the slope ${m}_{1}$ of the line passing through the points $(-2,6)$ and $(0,-6):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{m}_{1} & =\\frac{{y}_{2}-{y}_{1}}{{x}_{2}-{x}_{1}} \\\\  & =\\frac{-6-6}{0-(-2)} \\\\  & =\\frac{-12}{2} \\\\  & =-6\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince we want a perpendicular line, the slope $m$ of the line we're trying to find must satisfy\n\n$$\nm\\cdot (-6)=-1\\,⇒\\,m=\\frac{1}{6}.\n$$\n\nWe know that the line passes through the point $(-6,5).$ Using point-slope form and simplifying, we obtain the equation of the line:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y-{y}_{1} & =m(x-{x}_{1}) \\\\ y-5 & =\\frac{1}{6}(x-(-6)) \\\\ y-5 & =\\frac{1}{6}(x+6) \\\\ y-5 & =\\frac{1}{6}x+1 \\\\ y & =\\frac{1}{6}x+6\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186341834 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "460f3e7e-a692-5e84-84d3-bb04895e42c1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=2x+9" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186339750} {:answer/id #uuid "7de51bcc-dba4-5f66-9ed1-e89e2285349b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=x+3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186340264} {:answer/id #uuid "a496b65e-0fa0-5379-bb60-a181cb54a9be" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=\\frac{1}{2}x+6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186340623} {:answer/id #uuid "dc1a70a6-b427-555b-b378-aaf49435f770" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=x+7" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186341084} {:answer/id #uuid "f252e1db-2d4f-5cfe-b195-696e6f606c03" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=\\frac{1}{2}x-3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186341262}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "a496b65e-0fa0-5379-bb60-a181cb54a9be" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=\\frac{1}{2}x+6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186340623} :answer-field/id #uuid "f4e67ea3-0bdd-589c-834c-8bb372afcf1d" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186341816}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "99fe5e6a-20b3-53d0-a013-c1c2c0d3ad0c" :question/math-academy-id "q-72568" :question/problem "Find the equation of the line that passes through the point $(-2,5)$ and is perpendicular to the line passing through the points $(6,1)$ and $(4,5).$" :question/worked-solution "Perpendicular lines have slopes which are negative reciprocals of each other.\n\nFirst, let's find the slope ${m}_{1}$ of the line passing through the points $(6,1)$ and $(4,5):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{m}_{1} & =\\frac{{y}_{2}-{y}_{1}}{{x}_{2}-{x}_{1}} \\\\  & =\\frac{5-1}{4-6} \\\\  & =\\frac{4}{-2} \\\\  & =-2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince we want a perpendicular line, the slope $m$ of the line we're trying to find must satisfy\n\n$$\nm\\cdot (-2)=-1\\,⇒\\,m=\\frac{1}{2}.\n$$\n\nWe know that the line passes through the point $(-2,5).$ Using point-slope form and simplifying, we obtain the equation of the line:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y-{y}_{1} & =m(x-{x}_{1}) \\\\ y-5 & =\\frac{1}{2}(x-(-2)) \\\\ y-5 & =\\frac{1}{2}(x+2) \\\\ y-5 & =\\frac{1}{2}x+1 \\\\ y & =\\frac{1}{2}x+6\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186490396 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2034e111-3281-5462-b0b0-a7ccb1a7c133" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1 -x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186490394}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "2034e111-3281-5462-b0b0-a7ccb1a7c133" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1 -x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186490394} :answer-field/id #uuid "8d9a6db0-05c1-5d85-8a67-056821eb3220" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186490395}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "e6fe3232-91eb-5c5b-90f0-5931f279e031" :question/problem "What is the equation of the line that passes through the point $(0,1)$ and is perpendicular to the line passing through the points $(-1,1)$ and $(1,3)?$\n\n$y=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "Perpendicular lines have slopes that are negative reciprocals of each other.\n\nFirst, let's find the slope ${m}_{1}$ of the line passing through the points $(-1,1)$ and $(1,3):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{m}_{1} & =\\frac{{y}_{2}-{y}_{1}}{{x}_{2}-{x}_{1}} \\\\  & =\\frac{3-1}{1-(-1)} \\\\  & =\\frac{3-1}{1+1} \\\\  & =1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince we want a perpendicular line, the slope $m$ of the line we're trying to find must satisfy\n\n$$\nm\\cdot (1)=-1\\,⇒\\,m=-1.\n$$\n\nWe know that the line passes through the point $(0,1).$ Using point-slope form and simplifying, we obtain the equation of the line in slope-intercept form:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y-{y}_{1} & =m(x-{x}_{1}) \\\\ y-1 & =-1(x-0) \\\\ y-1 & =-x \\\\ y & =1 -x\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Equation of a Line Perpendicular to a Line Through Two Points"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186175924 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186175741 :question/id #uuid "49cee7e9-29a5-5e1e-bc13-7dbabaaea405" :question/math-academy-id "e-14730" :question/problem "What is the equation of the line that passes through the point $(6,2)$ and is perpendicular to $3x-2y=1?$" :question/worked-solution "First, we write the equation $3x-2y=1$ in its slope-intercept form as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3x-2y & =1 \\\\ -2y & =-3x+1 \\\\ y & =\\frac{3}{2}x-\\frac{1}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince perpendicular lines have slopes that are negative reciprocals of each other, the slope of the new line should be the negative reciprocal of the slope of the given line $y=\\dfrac{3}{2}x - \\dfrac{1}{2}.$ The given line has a slope of $\\dfrac{3}{2},$ so our perpendicular line must have a slope of $-\\dfrac{2}{3}.$\n\nOur perpendicular line must have the slope $m=-\\dfrac{2}{3}$ and pass through the point $(x_1,y_1) = (6,2).$ Using point-slope form and simplifying, we obtain the equation of the line in slope-intercept form:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y-{y}_{1} & =m(x-{x}_{1}) \\\\ y-2 & =-\\frac{2}{3}(x-6) \\\\ y-2 & =-\\frac{2}{3}x+4 \\\\ y & =-\\frac{2}{3}x+6\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "c52fd5dd-780e-5dab-805f-561383873dd4" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049201 :topic/math-academy-id 3725 :topic/title "Properties of Lines Given in Standard Form"}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186176417 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/id #uuid "c97302ea-f8e7-5c70-95e0-5cc0b820ce24" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186174740}] :question/id #uuid "a0a733e1-bbed-5414-830d-e413ae5ce0e3" :question/math-academy-id "q-214033" :question/problem "What is the equation of the line that passes through the point $(1,-5)$ and is perpendicular to $x + 3y = 12$?$y =$\n{{field-1}}"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186176432 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "b9a0fd16-a7fb-50f3-b7af-08c3f7c388b9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x + y =-5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186174773} {:answer/id #uuid "e444bbf2-3489-5620-aec6-98d5c66e7208" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2x - y =-4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186174774} {:answer/id #uuid "1698b28e-f5ca-5014-9c8d-6e6be465ad74" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2x + 3y = 0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186174775} {:answer/id #uuid "22bc909f-d369-586b-a41d-d055ee4efd3c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x + y =-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186174776} {:answer/id #uuid "48da768f-c47d-520e-b1b9-201676db3161" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x - y =-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186174777}] :answer-field/id #uuid "3db9b668-626e-53b0-85ef-b55b666f2a9c" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186174772}] :question/id #uuid "01fb643b-861f-5688-87eb-695c15d67442" :question/math-academy-id "q-23633" :question/problem "Find the equation of the line that passes through the point $(-3,-2)$ and is perpendicular to the line passing through the points $(2, 6)$ and $(-3, 1)$."} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186238009 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "08fb0cd4-c595-52e2-b7b8-f5921f1664ef" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3x+y=-4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186235858} {:answer/id #uuid "339a3f8d-ec60-53c0-b70c-2ad1dcb8beef" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3x-2y=-10" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186236143} {:answer/id #uuid "bf918fbb-8ce0-57e4-ba33-761c02c1700f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x+2y=2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186237036} {:answer/id #uuid "cd49d0d7-034b-5674-9bb1-76d06ca38e03" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x-2y=-6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186237119} {:answer/id #uuid "f90a028f-fc8e-54be-b155-96319e83f2b0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3x+2y=-2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186237390}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "f90a028f-fc8e-54be-b155-96319e83f2b0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3x+2y=-2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186237390} :answer-field/id #uuid "be8d6f01-6ff4-56d4-9645-4daf3987bc75" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186237683}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "5c2cb582-f805-50f4-ae14-8678782b90fa" :question/math-academy-id "q-103449" :question/problem "Find the equation of the line that passes through the point $(-2,2)$ and is perpendicular to the line passing through the points $(1,-1)$ and $(4,1).$" :question/worked-solution "Perpendicular lines have slopes which are negative reciprocals of each other.\n\nFirst, let's find the slope ${m}_{1}$ of the line passing through the points $(1,-1)$ and $(4,1):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{m}_{1} & =\\frac{{y}_{2}-{y}_{1}}{{x}_{2}-{x}_{1}} \\\\  & =\\frac{1-(-1)}{4-1} \\\\  & =\\frac{1+1}{4-1} \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince we want a perpendicular line, the slope $m$ of the line we're trying to find must satisfy\n\n$$\nm\\cdot (\\frac{2}{3})=-1\\,⇒\\,m=-\\frac{3}{2}.\n$$\n\nWe know that the line passes through the point $(-2,2).$ Using point-slope form and simplifying, we obtain the equation of the line:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y-{y}_{1} & =m(x-{x}_{1}) \\\\ y-2 & =-\\frac{3}{2}(x-(-2)) \\\\ y-2 & =-\\frac{3}{2}(x+2) \\\\ y-2 & =-\\frac{3}{2}x-3 \\\\ y & =-\\frac{3}{2}x-1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHowever, we want to write the equation in standard form. So, we will bring the $x$ term to the left-hand side and multiply both sides of the equation by $2:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =-\\frac{3}{2}x-1 \\\\ \\frac{3}{2}x+y & =-1 \\\\ 2\\cdot (\\frac{3}{2}x+y) & =2\\cdot (-1) \\\\ 3x+2y & =-2\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186290470 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1b9cfaf6-9a1a-5e8a-ba94-ac0ace22d609" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3x-2y=-5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186288209} {:answer/id #uuid "4b0ae802-61bf-59f5-8b33-d414ac86732a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x+y=0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186288552} {:answer/id #uuid "5a9e515d-8988-5114-8e78-b9e0df04aced" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2x+3y=1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186288663} {:answer/id #uuid "6463122b-ca6b-5780-a23a-d3c73e619707" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2x-3y=-5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186288741} {:answer/id #uuid "9a46eb07-3565-5f66-8f29-3f6a3b2e30be" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3x+2y=-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186289064}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "9a46eb07-3565-5f66-8f29-3f6a3b2e30be" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3x+2y=-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186289064} :answer-field/id #uuid "ec350e8b-707f-561e-b0a8-e7f213d56fbc" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186290223}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "96f3c704-d5cd-55de-a550-62294d23b6d6" :question/math-academy-id "q-23631" :question/problem "Find the equation of the line containing the point $(-1,1)$ that is perpendicular to $2x-3y=-5.$" :question/worked-solution "First, we write the equation $2x-3y=-5$ in its slope-intercept form, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2x-3y & =-5 \\\\ -3y & =-2x-5 \\\\ y & =\\frac{2}{3}x+\\frac{5}{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince perpendicular lines have slopes that are negative reciprocals of each other, the slope of the new line should be the negative reciprocal of the slope of the given line $y=\\frac{2}{3}x+\\frac{5}{3}.$ The given line has a slope of $\\frac{2}{3},$ so our perpendicular line must have a slope of $-\\frac{3}{2}.$\n\nOur perpendicular line must have the slope $m=-\\frac{3}{2}$ and pass through the point $({x}_{1},{y}_{1})=(-1,1).$ Using point-slope form and simplifying, we obtain the equation of the line:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y-{y}_{1} & =m(x-{x}_{1}) \\\\ y-1 & =-\\frac{3}{2}(x-(-1)) \\\\ y-1 & =-\\frac{3}{2}(x+1) \\\\ y-1 & =-\\frac{3}{2}x-\\frac{3}{2} \\\\ y & =-\\frac{3}{2}x-\\frac{1}{2} \\\\ 2y & =-3x-1 \\\\ 3x+2y & =-1\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186290500 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "45d37221-efd8-5394-a3bf-5cd387534c79" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{5}x+\\frac{19}{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186288506}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "45d37221-efd8-5394-a3bf-5cd387534c79" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{5}x+\\frac{19}{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186288506} :answer-field/id #uuid "66350c63-8aa4-5291-892a-860d45704838" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186289950}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "a0b26a33-738b-5140-8f04-e3933793a909" :question/math-academy-id "q-237230" :question/problem "What is the equation of the line that passes through the point $(-4,3)$ and is perpendicular to $5x+y=2?$\n\n$y=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "First, we write the equation $5x+y=2$ in its slope-intercept form as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}5x+y & =2 \\\\ y & =-5x+2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince perpendicular lines have slopes that are negative reciprocals of each other, the slope of the new line should be the negative reciprocal of the slope of the given line $y=-5x+2.$ The given line has a slope of $-5,$ so our perpendicular line must have a slope of $\\frac{1}{5}.$\n\nOur perpendicular line must have the slope $m=\\frac{1}{5}$ and pass through the point $({x}_{1},{y}_{1})=(-4,3).$ Using point-slope form and simplifying, we obtain the equation of the line in slope-intercept form:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y-{y}_{1} & =m(x-{x}_{1}) \\\\ y-3 & =\\frac{1}{5}(x-(-4)) \\\\ y-3 & =\\frac{1}{5}(x+4) \\\\ y-3 & =\\frac{1}{5}x+\\frac{4}{5} \\\\ y & =\\frac{1}{5}x+\\frac{19}{5}\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186290501 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "8a038781-f6d7-5ad5-aee2-0340c847e965" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-6x-42" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186288958}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "8a038781-f6d7-5ad5-aee2-0340c847e965" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-6x-42" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186288958} :answer-field/id #uuid "01a7209c-1afb-5996-b282-b1decb902920" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186289731}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "515eea7d-54c4-5f7a-8f42-e28d3350452e" :question/math-academy-id "q-237231" :question/problem "What is the equation of the line that passes through the point $(-7,0)$ and is perpendicular to $x-6y=-3?$\n\n$y=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "First, we write the equation $x-6y=-3$ in its slope-intercept form as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x-6y & =-3 \\\\ -6y & =-x-3 \\\\ y & =\\frac{1}{6}x+\\frac{1}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince perpendicular lines have slopes that are negative reciprocals of each other, the slope of the new line should be the negative reciprocal of the slope of the given line $y=\\frac{1}{6}x+\\frac{1}{2}.$ The given line has a slope of $\\frac{1}{6},$ so our perpendicular line must have a slope of $-6.$\n\nOur perpendicular line must have the slope $m=-6$ and pass through the point $({x}_{1},{y}_{1})=(-7,0).$ Using point-slope form and simplifying, we obtain the equation of the line in slope-intercept form:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y-{y}_{1} & =m(x-{x}_{1}) \\\\ y-0 & =-6(x-(-7)) \\\\ y & =-6(x+7) \\\\ y & =-6x-42\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186290502 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "7957bb33-46c9-58ef-8255-0b1ae5b1565b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{3}{4}x+\\frac{7}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186288868}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "7957bb33-46c9-58ef-8255-0b1ae5b1565b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{3}{4}x+\\frac{7}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186288868} :answer-field/id #uuid "0a3516f9-effa-5511-95b1-9b2d4cea03d5" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186289749}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "bd2e4750-a1c2-5012-bd49-32f6921fcef3" :question/math-academy-id "q-237232" :question/problem "What is the equation of the line that passes through the point $(1,1)$ and is perpendicular to $4x-3y=7?$\n\n$y=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "First, we write the equation $4x-3y=7$ in its slope-intercept form as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}4x-3y & =7 \\\\ -3y & =-4x+7 \\\\ y & =\\frac{4}{3}x-\\frac{7}{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince perpendicular lines have slopes that are negative reciprocals of each other, the slope of the new line should be the negative reciprocal of the slope of the given line $y=\\frac{4}{3}x-\\frac{7}{3}.$ The given line has a slope of $\\frac{4}{3},$ so our perpendicular line must have a slope of $-\\frac{3}{4}.$\n\nOur perpendicular line must have the slope $m=-\\frac{3}{4}$ and pass through the point $({x}_{1},{y}_{1})=(1,1).$ Using point-slope form and simplifying, we obtain the equation of the line in slope-intercept form:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y-{y}_{1} & =m(x-{x}_{1}) \\\\ y-1 & =-\\frac{3}{4}(x-1) \\\\ y-1 & =-\\frac{3}{4}x+\\frac{3}{4} \\\\ y & =-\\frac{3}{4}x+\\frac{7}{4}\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186292541 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "222e0bdd-c45c-5f9b-ae54-71cdb6646a9d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2x-y=2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186290780} {:answer/id #uuid "93fc4b47-47c7-5ebf-9229-bb1de3d6fc45" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2x+5y=26" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186291272} {:answer/id #uuid "96366abc-617a-5664-be0a-124e181d78a4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2x+y=10" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186291284} {:answer/id #uuid "d0dd3655-249b-55c7-ad7e-aa78ebaf2966" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x+2y=11" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186291519} {:answer/id #uuid "d8c2f176-99d3-5c0e-91c7-0a77ab6cde83" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x+4y=19" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186291538}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "d0dd3655-249b-55c7-ad7e-aa78ebaf2966" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x+2y=11" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186291519} :answer-field/id #uuid "f929ad97-39dd-50e0-b2f2-34f577615f96" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186292514}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "9bf24f67-6184-5bd5-8b16-92247aa73ac4" :question/math-academy-id "q-252524" :question/problem "Find the equation of the line that passes through the point $(3,4)$ and is perpendicular to the line passing through the points $(2,2)$ and $(5,8).$" :question/worked-solution "Perpendicular lines have slopes, which are negative reciprocals of each other.\n\nFirst, let's find the slope ${m}_{1}$ of the line passing through the points $(2,2)$ and $(5,8):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{m}_{1} & =\\frac{{y}_{2}-{y}_{1}}{{x}_{2}-{x}_{1}} \\\\  & =\\frac{8-2}{5-2} \\\\  & =\\frac{6}{3} \\\\  & =2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince we want a perpendicular line, the slope $m$ of the line we're trying to find must satisfy\n\n$$\nm\\cdot (2)=-1\\,⇒\\,m=-\\frac{1}{2}.\n$$\n\nWe know that the line passes through the point $(3,4).$ Using point-slope form and simplifying, we obtain the equation of the line:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y-{y}_{1} & =m(x-{x}_{1}) \\\\ y-4 & =-\\frac{1}{2}(x-3) \\\\ 2(y-4) & =-(x-3) \\\\ 2y-8 & =-x+3 \\\\ 2y & =-x+11 \\\\ y & =-\\frac{1}{2}x+\\frac{11}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHowever, we want to write the equation in standard form. So, we will bring the $x$ term to the left-hand side and multiply both sides of the equation by $2:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =-\\frac{1}{2}x+\\frac{11}{2} \\\\ \\frac{1}{2}x+y & =\\frac{11}{2} \\\\ 2\\cdot (\\frac{1}{2}x+y) & =2\\cdot \\frac{11}{2} \\\\ x+2y & =11\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186341836 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "3e1988b7-4bc8-5bda-86c5-639e0552dedd" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2x-3y=7" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186339683} {:answer/id #uuid "51dac7ff-2d97-5661-b543-ad769758a707" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x+y=6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186339880} {:answer/id #uuid "6d11b816-6f21-575c-a776-01785873cb84" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2x-3y=5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186340089} {:answer/id #uuid "803a7a88-7553-50e1-862d-0b786cfc737c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2x+3y=13" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186340279} {:answer/id #uuid "af6755f9-b09b-562d-a589-5067ac2ec549" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2x+3y=7" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186340728}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "803a7a88-7553-50e1-862d-0b786cfc737c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2x+3y=13" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186340279} :answer-field/id #uuid "58e7ea68-a247-56ce-a08d-252af6a5df70" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186341536}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "ad191a02-5856-5306-8980-ab9514c55d3b" :question/math-academy-id "q-72571" :question/problem "Find the equation of the line that passes through the point $(5,1)$ and is perpendicular to the line passing through the points $(1,5)$ and $(-3,-1).$" :question/worked-solution "Perpendicular lines have slopes which are negative reciprocals of each other.\n\nFirst, let's find the slope ${m}_{1}$ of the line passing through the points $(1,5)$ and $(-3,-1):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{m}_{1} & =\\frac{{y}_{2}-{y}_{1}}{{x}_{2}-{x}_{1}} \\\\  & =\\frac{-1-5}{-3-1} \\\\  & =\\frac{-6}{-4} \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince we want a perpendicular line, the slope $m$ of the line we're trying to find must satisfy\n\n$$\nm\\cdot (\\frac{3}{2})=-1\\,⇒\\,m=-\\frac{2}{3}.\n$$\n\nWe know that the line passes through the point $(5,1).$ Using point-slope form and simplifying, we obtain the equation of the line:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y-{y}_{1} & =m(x-{x}_{1}) \\\\ y-1 & =-\\frac{2}{3}(x-5) \\\\ y-1 & =-\\frac{2}{3}x+\\frac{10}{3} \\\\ y & =-\\frac{2}{3}x+\\frac{13}{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHowever, we want to write the equation in standard form. So, we will bring the $x$ term to the left-hand side and multiply both sides of the equation by $3:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =-\\frac{2}{3}x+\\frac{13}{3} \\\\ \\frac{2}{3}x+y & =\\frac{13}{3} \\\\ 3\\cdot (\\frac{2}{3}x+y) & =3\\cdot \\frac{13}{3} \\\\ 2x+3y & =13\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186481551 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "f1407e8e-66d1-5763-9a7a-aacc749f39bf" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{x}{5} + \\frac{9}{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186481561}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "f1407e8e-66d1-5763-9a7a-aacc749f39bf" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{x}{5} + \\frac{9}{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186481561} :answer-field/id #uuid "2abac703-59ec-5b3b-8e89-607f7dbded43" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186481547}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "52c85608-74fb-537e-b49a-ca47a66774d5" :question/problem "What is the equation of the line that passes through the point $(-4,1)$ and is perpendicular to $5x+y=2?$\n\n$y=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "First, we write the equation $5 x + y=2$ in its slope-intercept form as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}5 x + y=2 \\\\ y &= 2 -5 x \\\\ y &= 2 -5 x\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince perpendicular lines have slopes that are negative reciprocals of each other, the slope of the new line should be the negative reciprocal of the slope of the given line. The given line has a slope of $-5,$ so our perpendicular line must have a slope of $\\frac{1}{5}.$\n\nOur perpendicular line must have the slope $m=\\frac{1}{5}$ and pass through the point $(x_1,y_1)=(-4,1).$ Using point-slope form and simplifying, we obtain the equation of the line in slope-intercept form:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y-y_1 &= m(x-x_1) \\\\ y-(1) &= \\frac{1}{5}(x-(-4)) \\\\ y-(1) &= \\frac{x}{5} + \\frac{4}{5} \\\\ y &= \\frac{x}{5} + \\frac{9}{5}\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186481556 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1ea40432-27f5-51f8-970f-df3ccfdf6e6e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x + y=5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186481546} {:answer/id #uuid "5082d6c5-7fde-5faf-8116-aee1595931b9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2 x -y=-10" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186481550} {:answer/id #uuid "5ae72ad1-700c-5e57-823e-22a80c7c4423" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x + y=1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186481553} {:answer/id #uuid "5e38e4e5-46ed-537d-af50-ffa565441d62" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2 x + 3 y=18" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186481554} {:answer/id #uuid "bf84dd2e-d878-51ee-8dc8-9b699953659d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x -y=-7" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186481558}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "5ae72ad1-700c-5e57-823e-22a80c7c4423" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x + y=1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186481553} :answer-field/id #uuid "2b0130c6-29f1-5e19-8d71-e1e8a4138132" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186481548}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "80cf21cd-1688-5baa-bf78-1e05cbe212c4" :question/problem "Find the equation of the line that passes through the point $(-3,4)$ and is perpendicular to the line passing through the points $(2, 6)$ and $(-3, 1)$." :question/worked-solution "Perpendicular lines have slopes which are negative reciprocals of each other.\n\nFirst, let's find the slope ${m}_{1}$ of the line passing through the points $(2,6)$ and $(-3,1):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}m_1 &= \\frac{y_2-y_1}{x_2-x_1} \\\\ &= \\frac{1-(6)}{-3-(2)} \\\\ &= \\frac{-5}{-5} \\\\ &= 1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince perpendicular lines have slopes that are negative reciprocals of each other, the slope of the new line should be the negative reciprocal of the slope of the given line. The given line has a slope of $1,$ so our perpendicular line must have a slope of $-1.$\n\nOur perpendicular line must have the slope $m=-1$ and pass through the point $(x_1,y_1)=(-3,4).$ Using point-slope form and simplifying, we obtain the equation of the line in slope-intercept form:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y-y_1 &= m(x-x_1) \\\\ y-(4) &= -1(x-(-3)) \\\\ y-(4) &= -x -3 \\\\ y &= 1 -x\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHowever, we want to write the equation in standard form. So, we will bring the $x$ term to the left-hand side and multiply both sides of the equation by $1:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y &= 1 -x \\\\ x + y &= 1 \\\\ 1(x + y) &= 1(1) \\\\ x + y &= 1\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186481560 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "4a577eb1-695c-55d3-9958-742bb165e9fb" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2 x + 3 y=28" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186481549} {:answer/id #uuid "590f4196-5afc-597c-8846-19186099e725" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2 x + 3 y=22" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186481552} {:answer/id #uuid "5e450849-303a-5568-9866-2d4dcc0ac383" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2 x -3 y=-10" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186481555} {:answer/id #uuid "c74de8a8-dc89-584b-8e91-b63fb64475a7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2 x -3 y=-8" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186481559} {:answer/id #uuid "f51e9b94-ae4a-51bf-8264-6a0f64a4fde9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x + y=11" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186481562}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "4a577eb1-695c-55d3-9958-742bb165e9fb" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2 x + 3 y=28" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186481549} :answer-field/id #uuid "a04717ff-2885-50c6-ab11-6aff98029a55" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186481557}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "c7aa6290-5c44-5145-bf1e-dc13974023ae" :question/problem "Find the equation of the line that passes through the point $(5,6)$ and is perpendicular to the line passing through the points $(1,5)$ and $(-3,-1).$" :question/worked-solution "Perpendicular lines have slopes which are negative reciprocals of each other.\n\nFirst, let's find the slope ${m}_{1}$ of the line passing through the points $(1,5)$ and $(-3,-1):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}m_1 &= \\frac{y_2-y_1}{x_2-x_1} \\\\ &= \\frac{-1-(5)}{-3-(1)} \\\\ &= \\frac{-6}{-4} \\\\ &= \\frac{3}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince perpendicular lines have slopes that are negative reciprocals of each other, the slope of the new line should be the negative reciprocal of the slope of the given line. The given line has a slope of $\\frac{3}{2},$ so our perpendicular line must have a slope of $-\\frac{2}{3}.$\n\nOur perpendicular line must have the slope $m=-\\frac{2}{3}$ and pass through the point $(x_1,y_1)=(5,6).$ Using point-slope form and simplifying, we obtain the equation of the line in slope-intercept form:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y-y_1 &= m(x-x_1) \\\\ y-(6) &= -\\frac{2}{3}(x-(5)) \\\\ y-(6) &= \\frac{10}{3} -\\frac{2 x}{3} \\\\ y &= \\frac{28}{3} -\\frac{2 x}{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHowever, we want to write the equation in standard form. So, we will bring the $x$ term to the left-hand side and multiply both sides of the equation by $3:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y &= \\frac{28}{3} -\\frac{2 x}{3} \\\\ \\frac{2 x}{3} + y &= \\frac{28}{3} \\\\ 3(\\frac{2 x}{3} + y) &= 3(\\frac{28}{3}) \\\\ 2 x + 3 y &= 28\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Equation of a Line Perpendicular to a Line in Standard Form"}]}
